Chapter: Melsec

 MELSEC Communication (MC) protocol, the messaging that Mitsubishi
 SIMATIC-class PLCs (MELSEC-Q/L/iQ-R, and SLMP-compatible devices)
 speak on the factory floor -- the Mitsubishi counterpart to Rockwell's
 EtherNet/IP and Siemens' S7comm. This encodes the 3E binary request
 frame: a fixed subheader and access route, a request data length, a
 CPU monitoring timer, a command and subcommand, and device data. A
 device is a head number, a device code, and a point count. All
 multi-octet fields are little-endian. Each builder returns a
 List Integer.

 The request data length counts the monitoring timer, command,
 subcommand, and device data -- everything after the length field.

Section: Byte Helpers

  melsec-u16-le : Integer -> List Integer
  melsec-u16-le (v) = [bit-and v 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255]

  melsec-u24-le : Integer -> List Integer
  melsec-u24-le (v) =
   [bit-and v 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 16) 255]

Section: Commands and Devices

  melsec-cmd-batch-read : Integer = #0401
  melsec-cmd-batch-write : Integer = #1401
  melsec-sub-word : Integer = #0000
  melsec-sub-bit : Integer = #0001

  melsec-dev-d : Integer = #A8
  melsec-dev-m : Integer = #90
  melsec-dev-x : Integer = #9C
  melsec-dev-y : Integer = #9D
  melsec-dev-w : Integer = #B4
  melsec-dev-r : Integer = #AF

Section: Request Frame

 The 3E binary request opens with subheader 0x5000, a network number
 (0 for the own network), a PC number (0xFF for the own station), the
 request destination module I/O (0x03FF) and station (0), then the data
 length and the body. The monitoring timer 0x0010 is the common default.

  melsec-request : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  melsec-request (command) (subcommand) (payload) =
   let body = melsec-u16-le #0010 & melsec-u16-le command & melsec-u16-le subcommand & payload
   in [#50, #00, #00, #FF] & melsec-u16-le #03FF & [#00] & melsec-u16-le (list-length body) & body

Section: Device Access

 A device is addressed by a three-octet head number, a one-octet device
 code (0xA8 for a data register D, 0x90 for an internal relay M), and a
 two-octet point count.

  melsec-device : Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  melsec-device (dev-code) (head) (count) =
   melsec-u24-le head & [dev-code] & melsec-u16-le count

  melsec-words-le : List Integer -> List Integer
  melsec-words-le (words) = melsec-words-loop words 0 (list-length words) []

  melsec-words-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  melsec-words-loop (words) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else melsec-words-loop words (i + 1) len (acc & melsec-u16-le (list-at words i))

  melsec-batch-read-words : Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  melsec-batch-read-words (dev-code) (head) (count) =
   melsec-request melsec-cmd-batch-read melsec-sub-word (melsec-device dev-code head count)

  melsec-batch-write-words : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  melsec-batch-write-words (dev-code) (head) (words) =
   melsec-request melsec-cmd-batch-write melsec-sub-word (melsec-device dev-code head (list-length words) & melsec-words-le words)
